    conceptIds: ["suratha-samadhi","mahamaya","sadhana-samara"],
    translation: `There, near the Brāhmaṇa’s hermitage, the king saw a lone Vaiśya. Asked by the king — “Who are you, sir, and what brings you here? Why do you look so grief-stricken, so downcast?” — hearing these affectionate words, the Vaiśya, bowed in humility, answered the king: “I am Samādhi, a merchant born of a wealthy house. Cast out by wicked sons and wife greedy for riches, stripped of kin, wife and sons who seized my wealth, I came to the forest in grief, expelled by trusted kinsmen. I know nothing of my sons — whether welfare or calamity is theirs, whether their conduct is good or ill.” The king said: “Driven out by greedy sons, wife and kin for wealth — why does your heart still bind itself to them with love?” The Vaiśya said: “What you say of me, sir, is just as you say — yet what can I do? My heart will not harden toward them. Cast out by them, who abandoned a father’s love, greedy for riches — my wife abandoning a husband’s and kinsmen’s love — still on them alone my mind dwells in fondness. I do not understand this, O wise one, though I know it: how my heart overflows with love even for faithless kin. For them I sigh; gloom grips me. What can I do — my mind will not turn harsh toward the ungrateful?” Mārkaṇḍeya said: “Then the two together, O Brāhmaṇa, approached the sage — Samādhi the Vaiśya and that best of kings. After duly exchanging courtesies as deserved, the merchant and the monarch sat down and spoke awhile. ‘Lord, I would ask you one thing — tell me: what is it that pains my mind, when my heart of itself holds to nothing?’”`,
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (the two wounded seekers, beginner-full):**
1. **King = intellect, merchant = heart**: Suratha (rājasika-sattva, the ruling will) has lost his kingdom; Samādhi (the feeling heart, bruised into vairāgya) has lost his wealth and kin. Intellect asks the heart “why do you still love those who robbed you?” and the heart answers “I know — yet I cannot harden.” This dialogue is the living proof of dm-1-4: bondage is not ignorance but Mahāmāyā’s grip on the heart-knot (hṛdaya-granthi).
2. **Satsanga of fellow-sufferers**: Neither grieves alone any more; shared wound becomes shared enquiry (yathāvat pṛcchā). They approach Medhas (prajñā) together — intellect and feeling must both surrender before wisdom. Mark the courtesy (yathānyāya saṃvid): even broken kings keep dharma-manners; despair never excuses rudeness.
3. **The ripe question (ed29)**: “What pains my mind, when my heart holds to nothing?” — the seeker senses that suffering persists independent of objects. This is adhikāra ripeness: the question to which Medhas answers with Mahāmāyā (dm-1-54). For beginners: bring exactly this question to sādhanā — not “give me back my kingdom” but “why does loss still bind me?”

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
The meeting with the Vaishya Samadhi - himself driven from home by his own family, yet still worrying over their welfare - is read by every commentator as a deliberate structural parallel to Suratha's situation, proving the affliction is not particular to kingship but universal. Pradipa makes this the pivot of the whole opening narrative: two men from opposite ends of the social order (kshatriya sovereign and vaishya merchant), wronged in exactly opposite ways (one dispossessed by outsiders, one rejected by his own household), arrive at the identical symptom - grief they cannot rationally justify but also cannot dispel. Nagojibhatti's grammatical notes are used mainly to confirm Samadhi's vaishya identity from the compound describing his profession and dress. Caturdhari and Santanavi treat the dialogue between the two men as establishing shared confusion before either has met Medhas, so that the sage's coming teaching answers both men's questions - and, implicitly, the reader's - at once.`,
    keyPoints: ["**Two Seekers, One Wound**: Intellect (Suratha) and heart (Samādhi) suffer the same delusion from opposite sides.","**Knowing Yet Bound**: The heart confesses viveka without vairāgya — proof that bondage is Shakti, not mere ignorance.","**Shared Grief Becomes Enquiry**: Satsanga turns private pain into the ripe question Medhas can answer."]
