    conceptIds: ["mahamaya","cit-shakti","madhu-kaitabha","sadhana-samara"],
    translation: `Medhas said: “She is Knowledge itself — the eternal, unsurpassed ground of liberation — and She alone is also the ground of worldly bondage, the Empress of all lords. Lord, who indeed is this Goddess whom you call Mahāmāyā? How was She born, what are Her deeds, O twice-born? What is Her power, what Her nature, what Her origin — all that I wish to hear from you, O best of Brahman-knowers. She is eternal, the very form of the world, by whom all this is pervaded — yet hear from me Her manifold birth. Whenever, to accomplish the gods’ purpose, She manifests — though eternal — She is then said in the world to be ‘born.’ When, at the aeon’s end, Lord Viṣṇu embraced sleep as Yoga-nidrā, spreading His serpent-couch upon the world turned into a single ocean — then two dread demons, famed as Madhu and Kaiṭabha, born of Viṣṇu’s ear-wax, rose to slay Brahmā. Brahmā Prajāpati, seated on Viṣṇu’s navel-lotus, seeing those fierce demons and Janārdana sunk in sleep — with one-pointed heart he praised Yoganidrā, Her who dwells in Hari’s very eyes, so that (Viṣṇu) would wake.”`,
    commentary: `**Sādhana-Samara Commentary (Vidyā and avidyā are one Mother, beginner-full):**
1. **The whole Śākta creed in one verse (ed44)**: sā vidyā — She is liberating Knowledge; saṃsāra-bandha-hetu — She is also binding ignorance. One Cit-Śakti wears both faces (dm-1-54’s avidyā/vidyā-rūpa drawn out). Nāgojī and Guptavatī stress: She is sarveśvareśvarī — even Brahmā-Viṣṇu-Śiva are Her instruments, not Her masters.
2. **The disciple’s five questions (ed45-46)**: who is She? how born? what deeds? what power? what nature? what origin? Worldly curiosity asks for stories; adhikāra asks for tattva. Note the paradox Medhas will resolve (ed47-48): nityā (eternal) yet utpannā (born) — birth means manifestation for the gods’ work (avatāra-līlā), as Candraprabhā explains: the eternal sun is “born” each dawn for the seer’s eyes.
3. **Cosmic sleep and ear-wax demons (ed49-51)**: ekārṇava (single ocean) = consciousness undifferentiated; Viṣṇu (all-pervading Will) asleep = divine activity withdrawn; lotus-Brahmā = creative aspiration just sprouting; karṇa-mala (ear-wax) = stale hearing-habits, unexamined mental secretions. Madhu (sweet inertia) and Kaiṭabha (crooked obstinacy) attack aspiration at birth — the first battle every meditator knows: sleepiness and self-will strangling a fresh resolve.
4. **Praise, not fight (ed52)**: Brahmā cannot fight or wake Viṣṇu by force; with one-pointed heart (ekāgra — the whole sādhanā in one word) he praises the Sleep Herself dwelling in Hari’s eyes. Rule for beginners: when higher will sleeps, worship the veiling power to withdraw — śaraṇāgati, not struggle. Next (dm-1-66 existing): the great Brahma-stuti.

---
**Seven-Commentary Digest:**
The celebrated 'Ya devi sarva-bhuteshu... nidra-rupena samsthita' formula begins to take shape around this point, and here Medhas defines the Goddess philosophically as Vidya (true knowledge) who is simultaneously the cause of liberation and, in her other aspect, the cause of bondage - a paradox every commentator treats with real care since it forecloses any reading of Devi as simply benevolent in a narrow sense. Pradipa insists the two aspects (bandha and moksha) are not contradictory but sequential expressions of the same undivided power, comparable to how a single fire both burns and cooks depending on how it is used. Nagojibhatti's notes work through the precise philosophical vocabulary (mahamaya, avidya, vidya) to keep the passage consistent with Vedantic usage elsewhere in the commentarial tradition, while Guptavati links the passage forward to the Devi-sukta's more famous enumeration of the Goddess as the many faculties residing within all beings. The Hindi rendering keeps the paradox intact rather than softening it, true to the source.`,
    keyPoints: ["**One Mother, Two Works**: The same Mahamaya liberates as Vidya and binds as ignorance.","**Eternal Yet Born**: Nitya-Mother “takes birth” as avatara whenever the gods’ (faculties’) work demands.","**Ear-wax Demons**: Stale sense-habits attack fresh aspiration; praise — not force — wakes the sleeping Will."]
